---
export const prerender = true;
import Layout from '../../layouts/main.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../../components/CaseHeader.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import one from '../../assets/markup-1.png';
import two from '../../assets/markup-2.jpg';
import three from '../../assets/markup-3.jpg';
import four from '../../assets/markup-4.jpg';
import five from '../../assets/markup-5.jpg';
const images = [one, two, three, four, five];
const labels = [
  'Notes & preview',
  'Kanban board',
  'PDF annotation',
  'Mind map',
  'Whiteboard',
];
const descriptions = [
  'Markup note preview with subject folders and open document tabs',
  'Markup Kanban board with To Do, In Progress and Done columns',
  'Markup PDF reader with annotations and a comment sidebar',
  'Markup mind map with connected Header, Topic and Detail nodes',
  'Markup whiteboard with drawing tools, shapes and canvas settings',
];
---

<Layout
  title="Markup"
  description="A Markdown workspace with linked notes, maths, diagrams, cloud synchronisation and local files through Tauri."
>
  <article class="wrap page">
    <CaseHeader
      title="Markup"
      description="Markdown notes, diagrams and local files."
      stack="Next.js / TypeScript / CodeMirror / Tauri"
    >
      <a class="text-link" href="https://markup.freddiephilpot.dev">
        Open app ↗
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pphilfre/markup">
        Source ↗
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pphilfre/markup/releases">
        Desktop downloads ↗
      </a>
    </CaseHeader>
    <div class="gallery" data-gallery>
      <div class="gallery-images">
        {images.map((src, index) => (
          <figure hidden={index !== 0} data-slide>
            <a
              href={src.src}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              aria-label={
                'Open Markup screenshot ' + (index + 1) + ' at full size'
              }
            >
              <Image
                src={src}
                alt={descriptions[index]}
                widths={[480, 800, 1200, 1600]}
                sizes="(max-width: 700px) 90vw, 88vw"
                loading={index === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
                format="webp"
              />
            </a>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="gallery-controls">
        <p id="gallery-status" aria-live="polite">
          Notes & preview · 1 of 5
        </p>
        <div>
          {images.map((_, index) => (
            <button
              type="button"
              data-slide-button={index}
              data-label={labels[index]}
              aria-label={'Show screenshot ' + (index + 1)}
              aria-pressed={index === 0}
            >
              {index + 1}
            </button>
          ))}
        </div>
        <span>Click image to enlarge</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Why a workspace</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          I wanted somewhere to keep written notes, diagrams and document
          organisation together. Markdown is useful for the text, but a folder
          of files alone does not give me the same freedom to move between an
          explanation, a mind map and a whiteboard.
        </p>
        <p>
          Markup is my exploration of that flexible workspace across the web and
          desktop. My involvement includes shaping the workflow, configuring
          storage and authentication, testing behaviour and improving the
          application alongside implementation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Features</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Markup combines linked notes, maths and diagrams in a Markdown
          workspace. It supports cloud synchronisation, with local files
          available through the Tauri desktop app.
        </p>
        <p>
          Split editing and preview, folders, tabs and search keep documents
          manageable. Graph views connect linked notes, while mind maps and
          whiteboards provide space for ideas that do not fit a text document.
          Windows and macOS packages are available in the repository’s releases.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Implementation</h2>
      <div class="decisions">
        <div>
          <h3>Markdown editor</h3>
          <p>
            CodeMirror provides the editing foundation. Rendered maths and
            Mermaid diagrams sit alongside the text, while links and a graph
            view connect notes.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Web & desktop</h3>
          <p>
            Tauri adds access to local files. Workspace persistence and
            synchronisation connect that desktop workflow with the web
            application.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Storage</h3>
          <p>
            The source contains PostgreSQL through Prisma and a Convex sync
            path. Provider selection defaults to Convex; PostgreSQL is an
            alternative implementation. Zustand holds the workspace state, with
            local persistence for offline edits. WorkOS AuthKit handles sign-in
            for the authenticated workspace.
          </p>
          <a
            class="text-link"
            href="https://github.com/pphilfre/markup/blob/main/src/lib/db-provider.ts"
          >
            View provider selection ↗
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Local & remote data</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          The difficult part is coordinating data that can come from the local
          store, cloud queries and a desktop folder. Loading remote state must
          preserve local documents and open tabs, while applying incoming
          changes must not immediately trigger another save of the same data.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Convex sync path distinguishes hydration from edits, defers pushes
          while offline and flushes pending changes when connectivity returns.
          The desktop integration separately maps local documents to files such
          as <code>.md</code>, <code>.canvas</code> and <code>.mindmap</code>,
          then periodically reads external changes back into the workspace.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a practical sync implementation, with several sources of state
          to coordinate. I would not describe it as a guarantee that every
          simultaneous edit can be merged without conflict.
        </p>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://github.com/pphilfre/markup/blob/main/src/lib/convex-sync.tsx"
        >
          Read the sync implementation ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>What I’m learning</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Markup has helped me understand why storage is more than saving the
          current editor text. Document identity, workspace settings,
          authentication and the order in which data loads all affect whether
          the app behaves as expected.
        </p>
        <p>
          Using an established editor and rendering libraries lets me focus on
          how those parts work together. Testing the transitions between local,
          remote and desktop state has been as important as adding another
          editing feature.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <a class="next-project" href="/projects/dispatch">
      <span>Next project</span>
      <strong>
        <img src="/projects/icons/dispatch.svg" alt="" width="32" height="32" />
        Dispatch <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </strong>
    </a>
  </article>
</Layout>
<script>
  document
    .querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-gallery]')
    .forEach((gallery) => {
      const slides = gallery.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-slide]');
      const buttons = gallery.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(
        '[data-slide-button]',
      );
      buttons.forEach((button, index) =>
        button.addEventListener('click', () => {
          slides.forEach((slide, i) => (slide.hidden = i !== index));
          buttons.forEach((item, i) =>
            item.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(i === index)),
          );
          const status = gallery.querySelector('#gallery-status');
          if (status)
            status.textContent =
              button.dataset.label +
              ' · ' +
              (index + 1) +
              ' of ' +
              slides.length;
        }),
      );
    });
</script>
